%S A400944 1,1,1,1,5,1,1,4,4,1,1,11,53,11,1,1,7,223,223,7,1,1,17,115,475,115,17,
%T A400944 1,1,10,3523,549,549,3523,10,1,1,23,1391,30463,4309,30463,1391,23,1,1,
%U A400944 13,11257,27193,160123,160123,27193,11257,13,1,1,29,25559,441821,3643,497567,3643,441821,25559,29,1
%N A400944 Symmetric array read by ascending antidiagonals: A(n,m) = numerator(B(n,m)), where B is defined as B(n,1)=1, B(1,m)=1 and B(n,m) = 1 + (1/(n*m))*Sum_{j=1..n-1} Sum_{k=1..m-1} B(j,k), with m > 0.
%C A400944 B(n,m) is the expected number of subtractions in the algorithm starting from an ordered pair of positive integers (n,m), where, while n > 0 and m > 0, at each next step a pair (k_1,k_2) is randomly chosen such that k_1 in [n] and k_2 in [m], and the pair (n,m) is replaced with (n-k_1,m-k_2). See Erickson reference and link.
%C A400944 Beware that the Erickson book on page 132 contains a typo for n = m = 5: 4309/2300 instead of 4309/2400.
%D A400944 Martin Erickson, Beautiful Mathematics, Mathematical Association of America, 2011, pp. 131-132.
%H A400944 Stefano Spezia, <a href="/A400944/b400944.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..11325</a> (first 150 antidiagonals, flattened)
%H A400944 Martin Erickson, <a href="https://mathoverflow.net/questions/29604/two-dimensional-gobbling-algorithm">Two-Dimensional Gobbling Algorithm</a>
%F A400944 A(n,2) = A(2,n) = A165367(n-1).
%e A400944 The array of fractions begins as:
%e A400944   1,    1,       1,       1,         1, ...
%e A400944   1,  5/4,     4/3,    11/8,       7/5, ...
%e A400944   1,  4/3,   53/36, 223/144,    115/72, ...
%e A400944   1, 11/8, 223/144, 475/288,   549/320, ...
%e A400944   1,  7/5,  115/72, 549/320, 4309/2400, ...
%e A400944   ...
%t A400944 B[n_,1]:=1; B[1,m_]:=1; B[n_,m_]:=B[n,m]=1+Sum[Sum[B[j,k],{k,m-1}],{j,n-1}]/(n*m); A[n_,m_]:=Numerator[B[n,m]]; Table[A[n-m+1,m],{n,11},{m,n}]//Flatten
%Y A400944 1st column/row gives A000012.
%Y A400944 Cf. A165367, A400945 (denominators).
%K A400944 nonn,frac,tabl,new
%O A400944 1,5
%A A400944 _Stefano Spezia_, Oct 10 2026
